<div class="juris-body">
  <div class="juris-main">

    <!-- ═══ OVERVIEW ═══ -->
    <div id="panel-overview" class="tab-panel active">

      <div class="sec">
        <div class="nh-eyebrow">Civic Context · What FORA Holds Together</div>
        <h2 class="nh-headline">Two Causes of Cuban Suffering — Not One</h2>
        <p class="nh-lead">The Cuban crisis is argued through a single lens depending on who is speaking. American conservatives blame the communist government entirely. Some American progressives and young people blame the US embargo entirely. Both framings miss the documented reality — and both let the Cuban people down by making their suffering a proxy for someone else's argument.</p>
        <div class="context-box">
          <div class="cb-header">Two documented causes · FORA presents both as fact, not as political teams</div>
          <div class="cb-split">
            <div class="cb-col embargo">
              <div class="cb-col-label">Cause One</div>
              <div class="cb-col-title">The US Embargo — Real, 64 Years Old</div>
              <div class="cb-col-text">The US embargo restricts American trade, investment, and financial transactions with Cuba. Trump administration actions in 2026 blocked fuel deliveries and targeted GAESA specifically. These restrictions create genuine daily hardship for ordinary Cubans — not the ruling elite, who access hard currency regardless. The embargo has not achieved regime change in 64 years. Ordinary people absorb most of its costs.</div>
            </div>
            <div class="cb-divider"></div>
            <div class="cb-col gov">
              <div class="cb-col-label">Cause Two</div>
              <div class="cb-col-title">The Government — Acknowledged by Díaz-Canel Himself</div>
              <div class="cb-col-text">On June 18, 2026, Díaz-Canel told the Communist Party Central Committee that Cuba's crisis cannot be blamed on external pressure alone — citing "slowness, bureaucracy and norms that impede those who want to produce" and "decisions that we have put off." GAESA, the military conglomerate, holds an alleged $18–20B in hidden overseas accounts while Cubans experience fuel shortages and blackouts. These are government decisions, not embargo effects.</div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="quote-card">
          <button class="share-btn" onclick="foraShare(this)" title="Share" aria-label="Share"><svg width="13" height="13" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><circle cx="18" cy="5" r="3"/><circle cx="6" cy="12" r="3"/><circle cx="18" cy="19" r="3"/><line x1="8.59" y1="13.51" x2="15.42" y2="17.49"/><line x1="15.41" y1="6.51" x2="8.59" y2="10.49"/></svg></button>
          <div class="qc-text">"The situation calls for urgent and necessary changes... obstacles that don't come from outside, nor the blockade — slowness, bureaucracy and norms that impede those who want to produce, decisions that we have put off."</div>
          <div class="qc-attr">Miguel Díaz-Canel · Communist Party Central Committee · June 18, 2026</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="sec">
        <div class="nh-eyebrow">Breaking · June 18–19, 2026</div>
        <h2 class="nh-headline">Historic Reforms — Largest Market Opening Since 1959</h2>
        <p class="nh-lead">Cuba's Communist Party unanimously approved an emergency economic package featuring unprecedented free-market measures. Backed by Raúl Castro himself. The reforms target six core pillars. Details on specific regulations and implementation timelines have not been published as of June 19, 2026.</p>
        <div class="info-card" style="background:var(--teal-p);border-color:var(--teal-r);">
          <div class="ic-label" style="color:var(--teal);">Reform Pillars · Economic &amp; Social Program 2026</div>
          <div class="ic-val" style="font-size:12px;">Municipal Autonomy · State Enterprise Privatization · Agricultural Reform · Foreign Investment Opening · Private Real Estate Development · Cuban Diaspora Engagement · SME Expansion · Renewable Energy Transition</div>
        </div>
        <div class="note-card">
          <span class="nc-icon">⚠️</span>
          <span class="nc-text">Cuba has announced economic reforms before — 2010 "lineamientos," 2019 measures — with limited implementation. Independent analysts note the 2026 package announces intent without specific regulations or enforcement mechanisms. FORA tracks outcomes, not announcements. This page updates as implementation is documented.</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ═══ GOVERNMENT ═══ -->
    <div id="panel-government" class="tab-panel">

      <div class="sec">
        <div class="nh-eyebrow">Government · Structure</div>
        <h2 class="nh-headline">How Cuba Is Actually Governed</h2>
        <p class="nh-lead">Cuba is a one-party communist state in which the Communist Party of Cuba (PCC) holds constitutional supremacy over all government institutions. Elections are held, but all candidates must pass through a PCC-controlled nomination process. The National Assembly meets only twice a year; a 31-member Council of State governs the rest of the time. In practice, the Party preselects who may govern.</p>

        <table class="auth-table">
          <thead><tr>
            <th>Office</th><th>Selection</th><th>Current Holder</th><th>Note</th>
          </tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td><div class="at-office">President &amp; Party First Secretary</div><span class="at-badge ab-party">PCC Supreme</span></td>
              <td><div class="at-note">National Assembly vote · candidates preselected by PCC</div></td>
              <td><div class="at-holder">Miguel Díaz-Canel</div><div class="at-meta">Since 2018 · reelected Apr 2023</div></td>
              <td><div class="at-note">Handpicked successor to Raúl Castro. Does not hold supreme power in the manner of the Castros. US-sanctioned June 2026.</div></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><div class="at-office">Prime Minister</div><span class="at-badge ab-party">PCC</span></td>
              <td><div class="at-note">Designated by National Assembly on president's recommendation</div></td>
              <td><div class="at-holder">Manuel Marrero Cruz</div><div class="at-meta">Since Dec 2019</div></td>
              <td><div class="at-note">Former head of Gaviota tourism chain — a GAESA subsidiary. His appointment illustrates the FAR–government overlap.</div></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><div class="at-office">National Assembly President</div><span class="at-badge ab-party">PCC</span></td>
              <td><div class="at-note">Elected by Assembly deputies</div></td>
              <td><div class="at-holder">Esteban Lazo Hernández</div><div class="at-meta">Since Feb 2013</div></td>
              <td><div class="at-note">Assembly of 470 deputies — all PCC (442) or PCC-aligned independents (28). Meets twice per year.</div></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><div class="at-office">Council of State</div><span class="at-badge ab-unelect">31 Members</span></td>
              <td><div class="at-note">Elected by National Assembly</div></td>
              <td><div class="at-holder">Exercises legislative power between sessions</div><div class="at-meta">Governs ~363 days per year</div></td>
              <td><div class="at-note">The Assembly meets ~2 days per session, twice annually. The Council of State is the effective continuous government.</div></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><div class="at-office">Revolutionary Armed Forces (FAR)</div><span class="at-badge ab-unelect">Parallel Authority</span></td>
              <td><div class="at-note">Senior officers appointed through PCC military structures</div></td>
              <td><div class="at-holder">Controls GAESA economic empire</div><div class="at-meta">Estimated 37–40% of GDP</div></td>
              <td><div class="at-note">FAR operates GAESA independently of the civilian budget. No public audit has ever been conducted. Answerable only to PCC leadership.</div></td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>

        <div class="rubber-stamp">📋 How voting works in Cuba's National Assembly: Candidates are nominated by the National Candidature Commission — a PCC body. Municipal assemblies may reject individual candidates, but replacements come from a PCC reserve list. To be elected, a candidate needs more than 50% of valid votes in their constituency. All 470 candidates proposed in the 2023 elections were elected. Votes against or abstentions are recorded but have never changed an outcome. The 2023 election recorded the lowest voter turnout since 1993 — 75.8%.</div>
      </div>

      <div class="sec">
        <div class="nh-eyebrow">Legislation · What Cuba Actually Passes</div>
        <h2 class="nh-headline">The Legislative Record</h2>
        <p class="nh-lead">Cuba's National Assembly is the country's only legislative body, but it meets twice yearly for sessions typically lasting one to two days. Most major decisions are made by the Council of State or the Party between sessions and ratified by the Assembly. Votes are almost always unanimous. What Cuba passes is still consequential — it defines what is legal and what is criminal for 11 million people.</p>

        <div class="leg-row">
          <div class="leg-year">2026</div>
          <div class="leg-content">
            <div class="leg-title">Economic &amp; Social Program 2026 — Market Opening Package</div>
            <div class="leg-desc">Sweeping privatization reforms: state enterprise privatization, private real estate development, foreign investment opening, diaspora engagement, agricultural reform, SME expansion. The largest market opening since the 1959 revolution.</div>
            <div class="leg-badges"><span class="lb lb-pass">Passed</span><span class="lb lb-unan">Unanimous</span><span class="lb lb-econ">Economic</span></div>
            <div class="leg-engage">
              <button class="le-btn concur">Concur <span class="sca-count">847</span></button>
              <button class="le-btn object">Object <span class="sca-count">312</span></button>
              <button class="le-btn">Discuss</button>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="leg-vote">470–0<br><span style="font-size:7px;color:var(--ink-mut);">Jun 18, 2026</span></div>
        </div>

        <div class="leg-row">
          <div class="leg-year">2023</div>
          <div class="leg-content">
            <div class="leg-title">Social Communication Law (Ley 151)</div>
            <div class="leg-desc">Restricts speech and press freedoms by heavily limiting nonstate media. Establishes vague regulations governing online content. Actively enforced against journalists, activists, and ordinary citizens.</div>
