    <!-- ═══ ACTIVE CRISIS ═══ -->
    <div id="panel-crisis" class="tab-panel">

      <div class="sec">
        <div class="nh-eyebrow">Active Crisis · 2025–2026</div>
        <h2 class="nh-headline">Cuba's Worst Economic Crisis in Decades</h2>
        <p class="nh-lead">Cuba entered 2026 in its most severe economic contraction since the "Special Period" following the Soviet collapse in 1991. The proximate causes are the loss of Venezuelan oil, intensified US sanctions targeting GAESA, fuel scarcity, and rolling nationwide blackouts. The structural cause is a state-controlled economy that has not produced enough food, fuel, or basic goods for its population for years.</p>

        <div class="crisis-timeline">
          <div class="ct-item">
            <div class="ct-dot amber"></div>
            <div class="ct-date">Late 2025</div>
            <div class="ct-title">Venezuelan Oil Supply Ends</div>
            <div class="ct-text">The collapse of Venezuela's political arrangement under Maduro ended Cuba's primary oil supply, which had subsidized the island for two decades. Cuba scrambles for replacement fuel. Blackouts become acute — some neighborhoods lose power 12+ hours per day.</div>
          </div>
          <div class="ct-item">
            <div class="ct-dot red"></div>
            <div class="ct-date">January 2026</div>
            <div class="ct-title">Trump Blocks Fuel Deliveries · Mexico Pauses Shipments</div>
            <div class="ct-text">US pressure on fuel suppliers intensifies. Mexico temporarily halts oil shipments to Cuba after US pressure. Nicaragua cancels visa-free travel for Cuban citizens, closing a major migration route. Cuba faces a fuel emergency.</div>
          </div>
          <div class="ct-item">
            <div class="ct-dot amber"></div>
            <div class="ct-date">January 2026</div>
            <div class="ct-title">China Steps In — $80M Emergency Aid</div>
            <div class="ct-text">China approves $80M emergency financial aid plus 60,000 tonnes of rice. Chinese Ambassador: "We deeply understand that true friendship is revealed in times of greatest need." Russia pledges continued oil shipments.</div>
          </div>
          <div class="ct-item">
            <div class="ct-dot red"></div>
            <div class="ct-date">May 7, 2026</div>
            <div class="ct-title">US Sanctions GAESA</div>
            <div class="ct-text">The US formally designates GAESA under Executive Order 14404. Secretary Rubio calls GAESA "a military conglomerate that steals millions in aid for the Cuban people." GAESA Executive President Ania Guillermina Lastres Morera personally sanctioned. Cuba denies GAESA is to blame for the crisis.</div>
          </div>
          <div class="ct-item">
            <div class="ct-dot green"></div>
            <div class="ct-date">June 18, 2026</div>
            <div class="ct-title">Historic Economic Reforms Approved</div>
            <div class="ct-text">Communist Party unanimously approves sweeping privatization package backed by Raúl Castro. Díaz-Canel acknowledges domestic failures. This is the largest market opening in Cuba's revolutionary history. Implementation details remain unpublished.</div>
          </div>
          <div class="ct-item">
            <div class="ct-dot amber"></div>
            <div class="ct-date">Ongoing · June 2026</div>
            <div class="ct-title">Rolling Blackouts · Food Scarcity · Mass Emigration</div>
            <div class="ct-text">GDP contraction projected at 6.5%–15% for 2026. Hundreds of thousands have emigrated. Blackouts continue in most provinces. Basic goods remain scarce. Inflation is acute. The Cuban people absorb costs that are a combination of embargo, government dysfunction, and GAESA's extraction of national wealth into hidden accounts.</div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ═══ FOREIGN AFFAIRS ═══ -->
    <div id="panel-foreign" class="tab-panel">

      <div class="sec">
        <div class="nh-eyebrow">Foreign Affairs · The Full Picture</div>
        <h2 class="nh-headline">Cuba Is Not Isolated — It Is Contested</h2>
        <p class="nh-lead">American political discourse treats Cuba as isolated by the US embargo. The reality is more complex: Cuba has been deeply embedded in a network of geopolitical relationships for 65 years. Russia, China, Venezuela, and others have all been present — providing aid, investment, intelligence partnerships, and political cover. Those relationships have their own costs and conditions. Cuba's foreign affairs are not simply a story of American pressure; they are a story of superpower competition playing out on a small island.</p>

        <div class="partner-row">
          <div class="pr-flag">🇨🇳</div>
          <div class="pr-content">
            <div class="pr-name">China — Primary Patron as of 2025–2026</div>
            <div class="pr-role-badge rb-main">Strategic Partner</div>
            <div class="pr-text">Cuba joined China's Belt and Road Initiative in 2018. China is now Cuba's largest trading partner. In January 2026 Xi Jinping approved $80M emergency aid. In April 2026, the two nations signed 29 cooperation agreements covering credit financing, port infrastructure, technology transfer, and biotechnology. China is funding 55+ solar parks across Cuba targeting 2,000 MW — nearly two-thirds of Cuba's current electricity needs. Chinese solar exports to Cuba grew from $5M (2023) to $117M (2025). The Chinese Ambassador called the relationship an "iron friendship."</div>
            <div class="pr-stats">
              <span class="pr-stat"><strong>$80M</strong> emergency aid, Jan 2026</span>
              <span class="pr-stat"><strong>29</strong> cooperation agreements, Apr 2026</span>
              <span class="pr-stat"><strong>$117M</strong> solar exports, 2025</span>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="partner-row">
          <div class="pr-flag">🇷🇺</div>
          <div class="pr-content">
            <div class="pr-name">Russia — Historical Patron, Partially Stalled</div>
            <div class="pr-role-badge rb-ally">Long-Term Ally</div>
            <div class="pr-text">Russia replaced the USSR as Cuba's primary geopolitical patron after 1991. Putin pledged $1B+ in investment under "Plan 2030" in 2023 and continues oil shipments despite US pressure. Most Russian construction and infrastructure projects have stalled — Cuba cannot service debts, and Russia's resources are consumed by Ukraine. Russia's intelligence relationship with Cuba dates to the Soviet era and continues. Putin has publicly condemned US sanctions in 2026.</div>
            <div class="pr-stats">
              <span class="pr-stat"><strong>$1B+</strong> investment pledged (Plan 2030, 2023)</span>
              <span class="pr-stat"><strong>Most projects stalled</strong> — Ukraine war impact</span>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="partner-row">
          <div class="pr-flag">🇺🇸</div>
          <div class="pr-content">
            <div class="pr-name">United States — Adversary and Largest Remittance Source</div>
            <div class="pr-role-badge rb-embargo">Embargo · Since 1962</div>
            <div class="pr-text">The US maintains a comprehensive trade and financial embargo on Cuba, in effect since 1962. The Obama administration partially normalized relations (2014–2017); Trump reversed this in his first term and significantly escalated pressure in his second term (2025–). The US has simultaneously sanctioned GAESA, blocked fuel deliveries, and indicted former Cuban officials. The 1.8M Cuban-Americans in the US send billions in remittances to family on the island — making the US simultaneously Cuba's adversary and one of its largest sources of hard currency.</div>
            <div class="pr-stats">
              <span class="pr-stat"><strong>1962</strong> embargo start</span>
              <span class="pr-stat"><strong>1.8M</strong> Cuban-Americans</span>
              <span class="pr-stat"><strong>GAESA sanctioned</strong> May 7, 2026</span>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="partner-row">
          <div class="pr-flag">🇻🇪</div>
          <div class="pr-content">
            <div class="pr-name">Venezuela — Former Oil Lifeline, Now Disrupted</div>
            <div class="pr-role-badge rb-complex">Relationship Disrupted</div>
            <div class="pr-text">Venezuela under Maduro provided subsidized oil to Cuba for over two decades in exchange for Cuban doctors and security advisors — a relationship that propped up both governments. The collapse of Venezuela's political arrangement in late 2025 ended this lifeline, and the loss of Venezuelan oil is the primary trigger of Cuba's current fuel emergency. The Cuba–Venezuela relationship illustrates how authoritarian governments sustain each other — and how dependent that can make their people on political outcomes beyond their control.</div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ═══ DOCTRINE & HISTORY ═══ -->
    <div id="panel-doctrine" class="tab-panel">

      <div class="sec">
        <div class="nh-eyebrow">Doctrine · The Ideas Cuba Governs By</div>
        <h2 class="nh-headline">Marxism-Leninism, Cuban Style</h2>
        <p class="nh-lead">Cuba's governing ideology draws on Marxism-Leninism as interpreted through the Cuban revolutionary experience — particularly Fidel Castro's synthesis of socialist economics, anti-imperialism, and national sovereignty. It has been adapted repeatedly to survive political shocks: the Soviet collapse, the Special Period, Raúl's partial reforms, and now Díaz-Canel's 2026 market opening.</p>

        <div class="doctrine-card">
          <button class="share-btn" onclick="foraShare(this)" title="Share" aria-label="Share"><svg width="13" height="13" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><circle cx="18" cy="5" r="3"/><circle cx="6" cy="12" r="3"/><circle cx="18" cy="19" r="3"/><line x1="8.59" y1="13.51" x2="15.42" y2="17.49"/><line x1="15.41" y1="6.51" x2="8.59" y2="10.49"/></svg></button>
          <div class="dc-eyebrow">Core Doctrine</div>
          <div class="dc-title">The Four Pillars of Cuban Revolutionary Ideology</div>
          <div class="dc-text">
            <strong>1. Anti-imperialism</strong> — Cuba frames its entire political existence as resistance to US imperialism. The embargo is the central evidence for this narrative and is used to justify restrictions on civil liberties as "wartime necessities."<br><br>
            <strong>2. Socialist Economics</strong> — State control of the means of production; social ownership; universal healthcare and education. Cuba has genuine achievements in these areas that coexist with genuine failures in food, housing, and living standards.<br><br>
            <strong>3. National Sovereignty</strong> — Cuba frames any external criticism as an attack on its sovereignty. This framing has been used to reject human rights pressure from the UN, the US, and Latin American neighbors.<br><br>
            <strong>4. Fidelismo</strong> — Fidel Castro's personal authority created a governing style based on charisma, revolutionary legitimacy, and mass mobilization. Díaz-Canel lacks this. His 2026 admission of domestic failures is a departure from the Fidelista tradition of blaming external enemies.
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="nh-eyebrow" style="margin-top:24px;">History · A Civilization Timeline</div>
        <div class="hist-item">
          <div class="hi-year">1492</div>
          <div class="hi-content"><div class="hi-title">Columbus arrives. Indigenous Taíno population decimated within decades.</div><div class="hi-text">Cuba becomes Spain's most valuable Caribbean colony. Sugar and tobacco plantations built on enslaved African labor — a system that defines the island's ethnic and economic DNA for centuries.</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="hist-item">
          <div class="hi-year">1868</div>
          <div class="hi-content"><div class="hi-title">Ten Years' War — First War of Independence</div><div class="hi-text">Cuban rebels fight Spain for independence. War ends with neither victory nor freedom. Seeds of revolutionary consciousness planted.</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="hist-item">
          <div class="hi-year">1898</div>
          <div class="hi-content"><div class="hi-title">Spanish-American War · Cuba "freed" — then occupied</div><div class="hi-text">The US intervenes, defeats Spain, and occupies Cuba. The Platt Amendment grants the US the right to intervene in Cuban affairs at will. Cuban independence is nominal for decades.</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="hist-item">
          <div class="hi-year">1959</div>
          <div class="hi-content"><div class="hi-title">Revolution. Castro takes power. Batista flees.</div><div class="hi-text">Fidel Castro's 26th of July Movement overthrows US-backed dictator Fulgencio Batista. Revolution is initially broadly popular. Rapid nationalization of industry alienates the middle class; hundreds of thousands emigrate.</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="hist-item">
          <div class="hi-year">1961</div>
          <div class="hi-content"><div class="hi-title">Bay of Pigs. Cuba declares itself socialist.</div><div class="hi-text">US-sponsored invasion fails catastrophically, cementing Castro's legitimacy. Cuba announces it is a socialist state and aligns with the Soviet Union.</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="hist-item">
          <div class="hi-year">1991</div>
          <div class="hi-content"><div class="hi-title">Soviet collapse. The Special Period begins.</div><div class="hi-text">Cuba loses ~80% of its import capacity overnight. Food rationing intensifies. The government introduces limited market reforms to survive. GAESA is founded to give the military a hard-currency base.</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="hist-item">
          <div class="hi-year">2021</div>
          <div class="hi-content"><div class="hi-title">July 11 Protests — Largest in 60 years</div><div class="hi-text">Mass protests in all Cuban provinces — unprecedented in scope. Chants of "Patria y Vida" ("Homeland and Life") replace the official slogan "Patria o Muerte." Hundreds arrested. Government restores internet access only after international pressure.</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="hist-item">
          <div class="hi-year">2026</div>
          <div class="hi-content"><div class="hi-title">Historic market reforms approved · June 18</div><div class="hi-text">Communist Party unanimously approves the largest economic opening since the revolution — acknowledging domestic failures for the first time. Whether implementation follows is Cuba's defining question.</div></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

